## 3. SOAP API## Standard Web Request Structure

POST /ws/OrderService.asmx HTTP/1.1
Host: ://target.com
Content-Type: text/xml; charset=utf-8
SOAPAction: "http://tempuri.org"

<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<soap:Envelope xmlns:soap="http://xmlsoap.org">
  <soap:Body>
    <CreateOrder xmlns="http://tempuri.org">
      <ItemID>730</ItemID>
      <Quantity>1</Quantity>
    </CreateOrder>
  </soap:Body>
</soap:Envelope>

## Vulnerabilities & How to Check Them

* XXE (XML External Entity Injection):
* The Vulnerability: The XML handling software engine on the backend processes external reference files blindly.
   * How to Test: Define a custom entity pointing to an internal file or a controlled server layout at the very top of the XML body:
   
   <!DOCTYPE test [ <!ENTITY xxe SYSTEM "file:///etc/passwd"> ]>
   
   Then reference it inside a text field: <ItemID>&xxe;</ItemID>. If the file contents print out in the server error or response, XXE is confirmed. [20, 21, 22, 23] 
* WSDL Information Disclosure:
* The Vulnerability: Leaving the map of the entire API structure publicly readable.
   * How to Test: Append ?wsdl or ?singleWsdl to the end of the endpoint URL path. This exposes a massive configuration layout mapping every hidden feature name available. [24] 

